{"meta":{"course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"01","section":"02","chapter_title":"Foundations of AI Ethics","title":"Module 2: Applying Ethics Frameworks to AI","description":"Triangulate AI deployment decisions across consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics, and care ethics; recognize where the frameworks converge (permission to proceed) and where they diverge (the live decision).","learning_objectives":["Apply each of four ethical frameworks (consequentialist, deontological, virtue, care) to a deployment decision.","Identify each framework's diagnostic question, strengths, and characteristic blind spots.","Distinguish convergence (defensible) from disagreement (where the live ethical choice lives).","Reject the move of hiding a contested decision behind any single framework's verdict."],"concept_ids":["consequentialism","deontology","virtue_ethics","care_ethics","ethical_triangulation"],"prereq_section_ids":["ai-ethics/01/01"],"practice_ids":["ethics_aggregation_blindspot","ethics_virtue_question","ethics_care_focus","ethics_triangulation_value"],"estimated_time_minutes":13,"order":2},"content":"# Four Ethical Frameworks for AI Decisions\n\n> **\"There is no view from nowhere. Every evaluation of a technology smuggles in a theory of what matters.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Opening: One Tool, Four Verdicts\n\nIn 2018, Amazon quietly scrapped an experimental resume-screening tool after engineers discovered it had taught itself to penalize resumes containing the word \"women's\" (as in \"women's chess club captain\") and to downgrade graduates of two all-women colleges (reported by Reuters, October 2018). The model had been trained on a decade of the company's own hiring decisions, which skewed male, so it learned to reproduce that skew.\n\nNow ask the deceptively simple question: *was building that tool wrong?* Notice that your answer depends entirely on what you measure it against.\n\n- If you judge by **outcomes**, the tool was wrong because it produced discriminatory results, full stop.\n- If you judge by **duties and rights**, it was wrong even before any biased hire occurred, because it treated applicants as data points to be sorted rather than persons owed equal consideration.\n- If you judge by **character**, the deeper failure was an engineering culture that treated \"automate hiring\" as a purely technical optimization problem.\n- If you judge by **relationships and vulnerability**, the wrong fell hardest on a group with little power to contest an opaque automated rejection.\n\nEach of these is a recognizable ethical framework, and each picks out something the others miss. This is why a deployment answer rooted in only one framework is fragile: using a single lens means quietly inheriting that lens's blind spots. The defensible move is to **triangulate**: state what each major framework says, see where they converge, and treat the points of disagreement as the live decision.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Frameworks are not interchangeable opinions to pick from. They are instruments, each sensitive to a different kind of harm. A complete ethical analysis runs all of them and reports where they conflict, rather than choosing the one that endorses the decision you already wanted.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** the four major ethical frameworks (consequentialism, deontology, virtue ethics, care ethics) by the question each one asks.\n2. **Identify** the characteristic blind spot of each framework.\n3. **Apply** all four frameworks to a concrete AI deployment using a structured stakeholder and tradeoff analysis.\n4. **Locate** the point of disagreement among frameworks and explain why that disagreement, not any single verdict, is the real decision.\n5. **Critique** the triangulation method itself, recognizing its limits.\n\n---\n\n## Consequentialism: Judge by Outcomes\n\nConsequentialism evaluates an action by its consequences. The most common variant, **utilitarianism** (Bentham, Mill), maximizes aggregate well-being. In AI, consequentialist reasoning shows up whenever someone says \"the model is good if it does more good than harm in the population it serves.\"\n\n**Strengths.** Tractable. You can quantify and aggregate. It scales naturally to the millions of decision subjects an AI system affects, and it forces explicit accounting of real-world effects rather than intentions.\n\n**Weaknesses.** Aggregation hides distribution. A model that helps 95% of users a little and harms 5% catastrophically can score well on aggregate metrics. Consequentialism alone tends to under-weigh concentrated harm to small groups, and it is only as honest as the metric chosen to stand in for \"well-being.\"\n\n**Diagnostic question:** *Whose well-being are we summing, and whose is being averaged out?*\n\n---\n\n## Deontology: Judge by Duties and Rights\n\nDeontology (Kant and successors) evaluates an action by whether it respects rules, duties, and rights, regardless of outcomes. In AI, deontological reasoning shows up in privacy claims, in informed-consent requirements, in the \"right to explanation\" debate (GDPR Article 22), and in the principle that a person should not be **used merely as a means** for others' benefit.\n\n**Strengths.** Protects individuals from being sacrificed to aggregate goals. Resists the consequentialist trap of \"5% catastrophic harm is acceptable if 95% benefit.\" Aligns with rights-based legal frameworks.\n\n**Weaknesses.** Can be rigid. Rules that work in normal cases sometimes produce absurd results in edge cases. Tells you what you cannot do, often without telling you what to do, and offers little guidance when two duties (say, privacy and safety) genuinely collide.\n\n**Diagnostic question:** *Whose rights or autonomy is the system overriding, and is the override justified by anything other than aggregate benefit?*\n\n---\n\n## Virtue Ethics: Judge by Character\n\nVirtue ethics (Aristotle, recently extended to technology by Shannon Vallor in *Technology and the Virtues*) asks not \"what action is right?\" but \"what would a person of good character do, and what habits does this technology cultivate?\" In AI, virtue-ethics reasoning shows up when someone asks whether a recommendation system is *making us better at thinking* or worse at it, whether a chatbot tutor is *cultivating curiosity* or *training dependence*.\n\n**Strengths.** Captures long-term effects on the people who use the technology. Names the worry about deskilling, manipulation, and erosion of judgment that the other frameworks struggle to articulate.\n\n**Weaknesses.** Specifying which virtues matter is itself contested. Different cultural traditions emphasize different virtues. Virtue ethics is harder to operationalize in design reviews because it resists checklists.\n\n**Diagnostic question:** *What habits and dispositions does this system cultivate in its users over months and years?*\n\n---\n\n## Care Ethics: Judge by Relationships and Vulnerability\n\nCare ethics (Gilligan, Noddings, Held, Tronto) evaluates an action by how it attends to the vulnerable and to the relationships in which people are embedded. In AI, care-ethics reasoning shows up most strongly when the decision subject is a child, a patient, an elderly person, or anyone with limited capacity to advocate for themselves.\n\n**Strengths.** Foregrounds power asymmetry and dependency that the other three frameworks treat as background. Aligns with educational ethics, medical ethics, and the special protections for minors that recur in AI deployments.\n\n**Weaknesses.** Less prescriptive than deontology. Risks paternalism if \"caring for\" slides into \"deciding for.\" Can be hard to scale to anonymous, population-level systems where no concrete relationship exists.\n\n**Diagnostic question:** *Who is most vulnerable in this deployment, and how does the system attend to their needs and to the relationships they depend on?*\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"ethics-framework-quad\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## At a Glance: Four Lenses\n\n| Framework | Core Question | What It Catches | Characteristic Blind Spot |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Consequentialism | Do the outcomes maximize well-being? | Net real-world harm and benefit | Concentrated harm hidden by averages |\n| Deontology | Are rights and duties respected? | Rights violations, lack of consent | Rigidity; duty conflicts |\n| Virtue Ethics | What character does this cultivate? | Deskilling, dependence, manipulation | Contested, hard to operationalize |\n| Care Ethics | Are the vulnerable attended to? | Power asymmetry, dependency | Paternalism; hard to scale |\n\nThe fastest way to keep the four lenses straight is by the diagnostic question each one asks. Match each framework to its question; if you can recall the question, you can run the lens.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Consequentialism\",\"definition\":\"Whose well-being are we summing, and whose is being averaged out?\"},{\"term\":\"Deontology\",\"definition\":\"Whose rights or autonomy is the system overriding, and is the override justified by more than aggregate benefit?\"},{\"term\":\"Virtue ethics\",\"definition\":\"What habits and dispositions does this system cultivate in users over months and years?\"},{\"term\":\"Care ethics\",\"definition\":\"Who is most vulnerable here, and how does the system attend to their needs and relationships?\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reviewer says a tutoring chatbot is fine because students who use it score higher on the next quiz. Which framework's blind spot is this argument most at risk of falling into?\" explanation=\"Judging only by the measured outcome (quiz score) is a consequentialist move, and its characteristic blind spot is missing what the metric leaves out. A virtue-ethics lens would ask whether the bot cultivates genuine understanding or trains dependence that the quiz score cannot see.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Consequentialism, by trusting a single outcome metric and missing cultivated habits like dependence.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Care ethics, because it ignores rights.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Deontology, because it counts consequences.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Applying the Frameworks: A Triangulation Workflow\n\nFor a real deployment decision, run all four lenses and watch where they converge or disagree.\n\n1. **Consequentialist pass.** Estimate aggregate benefits and harms across all stakeholder groups (decision-maker, decision subject, data subject, bystanders). Note the distribution, not just the average.\n2. **Deontological pass.** Identify which rights or duties the system overrides. Is informed consent meaningful? Is anyone being used merely as a means?\n3. **Virtue pass.** Project the system's effect on user habits over months. Does it cultivate the capacities the deployment claims to support, or erode them?\n4. **Care pass.** Identify the most vulnerable parties. Does the system attend to their dependency, or amplify it?\n\n**Convergence is permission to proceed.** When all four frameworks point the same way, the decision is defensible.\n\n**Disagreement is the live decision.** When frameworks diverge, common in real cases, that disagreement *is* the ethical choice the team must make. It cannot be hidden behind any single framework's verdict.\n\n---\n\n## A Worked Example: Recommender System for Adolescent Users\n\nConsider a recommender for a video platform with many users aged 13 to 17. We will apply each lens in turn, then summarize in a decision table.\n\nBefore you read the four passes, predict how each lens will land on shipping the recommender as-is. The verdicts are not unanimous, and the split is the whole lesson.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict each framework's verdict on the adolescent recommender\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Aggregate engagement is up; average well-being is mildly positive but a minority is seriously harmed. What does the CONSEQUENTIALIST pass conclude about shipping as-is?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Lean yes: the aggregate is net positive\",\"No: any harm is disqualifying\",\"It cannot say anything\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Consequentialism leans yes because the population average is positive. Its blind spot is exactly here: the concentrated tail harm gets averaged away.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Minors cannot give meaningful informed consent to deep behavioral profiling. What does the DEONTOLOGICAL pass say?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, consent is irrelevant\",\"No: meaningful consent is absent and minors are used as a means to an engagement target\",\"Concern, but no verdict\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Deontology says no. Consent is not meaningful for minors, and treating them merely as a means to an engagement metric violates a duty regardless of the positive aggregate.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The VIRTUE pass registers a concern (the system cultivates passive consumption and shorter attention, not the discernment the platform advertises). The CARE pass says no: adolescents are developmentally vulnerable and the persuasion mechanics, tuned for adults, ignore that vulnerability. Tally: one lean-yes, two no, one concern. The lone yes comes from the lens most prone to hiding concentrated harm, which is why that three-against-one split is the live decision, not a vote to be counted.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n**Consequentialist pass.** Aggregate engagement metrics show the system increases time on platform. Aggregate well-being is harder; some users report better content discovery, others report sleep loss and anxiety. The average is mildly positive, but the variance is concentrated: a minority experience serious harm.\n\n**Deontological pass.** Users did not give informed consent to behavioral profiling at the depth the system uses. COPPA (the US Children's Online Privacy Protection Act) does not cover ages 13 and up, but the autonomy concerns it codifies arguably do. Minors are being treated as a means to an engagement target.\n\n**Virtue pass.** The system cultivates passive consumption habits and shorter attention windows. Whether this counts as virtue erosion depends on what you think the platform is for, but at minimum it does not cultivate the discernment the platform's marketing claims.\n\n**Care pass.** Adolescents are developmentally vulnerable. The system's persuasion mechanics, designed and tuned for general adult engagement, fail to attend to that specific vulnerability.\n\n### Stakeholder and Tradeoff Table\n\n| Stakeholder | Primary interest | Helped by shipping as-is? | Most relevant lens |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Adolescent user | Well-being, autonomy, healthy habits | Mixed; harmed at the tail | Care, virtue |\n| Parent / guardian | Child safety, informed oversight | No; consent is not meaningful | Deontology, care |\n| Platform (business) | Engagement, ad revenue | Yes | Consequentialism |\n| Advertisers | Reach, attention | Yes | Consequentialism |\n| General adult users | Content discovery | Mostly yes | Consequentialism |\n\n### Where the Frameworks Land\n\n| Framework | Verdict on shipping as-is |\n| --- | --- |\n| Consequentialism | Lean yes (aggregate benefit positive) |\n| Deontology | No (consent not meaningful for minors) |\n| Virtue ethics | Concern (cultivates passive habits) |\n| Care ethics | No (ignores adolescent vulnerability) |\n\n**Where the frameworks converge:** the system needs design changes that limit persuasion mechanics for adolescent accounts.\n\n**Where they disagree:** the consequentialist case for shipping (aggregate benefit positive) collides with the deontological and care-ethics cases for not shipping until consent and vulnerability are addressed. That three-against-one split, with the lone \"yes\" coming from the framework most prone to hiding concentrated harm, is the live decision.\n\nThe workflow does not produce a single verdict. It produces a structured map of where the team's judgment is being applied, so that judgment can be defended later.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Ethics Frameworks Applied\" context=\"A startup is offered access to a hospital system's electronic health records to train an AI triage model. The data includes some unconsented subjects (deceased patients, edge cases). The team must decide whether to proceed. Apply all four frameworks (consequentialist, deontological, virtue, care) and identify where they converge and where they disagree.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\nWalk that triage decision yourself. Each choice below commits you to a framework's logic and lands you in its characteristic strength or blind spot. There is no costless path; the point is to see which concern you are trading away.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The EHR triage model: should the startup proceed?\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You can train a triage model that may speed care for thousands, but the dataset includes records of patients who never consented (deceased patients, edge cases). On what basis do you decide?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Net lives saved outweighs the consent gap; proceed\",\"next\":\"conseq\",\"feedback\":\"You reason from outcomes.\"},{\"label\":\"Using data from people who never consented is a rights violation; pause\",\"next\":\"deont\",\"feedback\":\"You reason from duties and rights.\"},{\"label\":\"Ask who is most vulnerable and dependent in this pipeline first\",\"next\":\"care\",\"feedback\":\"You reason from care and vulnerability.\"}]},\"conseq\":{\"prompt\":\"You proceed on the aggregate-benefit case. A journalist asks how you justify using data from people who could not consent. What is the honest weakness of your position?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Consequentialism averaged away a concentrated rights harm to the unconsented\",\"next\":\"conseq_end\",\"feedback\":\"You name your own blind spot.\"},{\"label\":\"There is no weakness; the math is positive\",\"next\":\"gamed\",\"feedback\":\"You declare convergence without testing it.\"}]},\"conseq_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: the outcome case can be real AND the rights objection can stand. A severe rights violation can outweigh a large diffuse benefit; counting one yes is not permission. Run the deontological pass before you ship.\",\"feedback\":\"This is why triangulation is not a single-lens decision.\",\"choices\":[]},\"deont\":{\"prompt\":\"You pause on consent grounds. Hospital leadership notes the model could reduce triage deaths now. A pure deontology stance can be rigid. What is the defensible next step?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Seek a consent-respecting path: IRB review, de-identification, a waiver process, or consented data only\",\"next\":\"deont_end\",\"feedback\":\"You convert a hard no into a constructive condition.\"},{\"label\":\"Refuse all use forever, no exceptions\",\"next\":\"rigid\",\"feedback\":\"You apply the rule rigidly.\"}]},\"deont_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: deontology protects the unconsented from being used merely as a means, but its rigidity is checked by finding a rights-respecting route to the benefit rather than abandoning it. Where consequentialism (proceed) and deontology (do not use unconsented data) collide is the live decision.\",\"feedback\":\"You made the conflict visible and attributable instead of hiding it.\",\"choices\":[]},\"care\":{\"prompt\":\"The most vulnerable are incapacitated and deceased patients with no advocate, plus future patients the model will triage. How does the care lens shape the decision?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Build in advocacy for those who cannot consent and guardrails for those the model will judge\",\"next\":\"care_end\",\"feedback\":\"You attend to dependency and power asymmetry.\"}]},\"care_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: care ethics foregrounds the parties with no power to contest the deployment. It converges with deontology on protecting the unconsented and adds attention to the dependent future patients, which the consequentialist tally treated as background.\",\"feedback\":\"Two lenses converge here; that convergence strengthens the case for conditions before proceeding.\",\"choices\":[]},\"gamed\":{\"prompt\":\"Declaring \\\"the math is positive, no weakness\\\" is exactly how triangulation gets gamed: a shallow pass that endorses the decision you already wanted. Honest triangulation requires hunting the strongest objection each lens can raise.\",\"feedback\":\"Re-run the passes adversarially before you proceed.\",\"choices\":[]},\"rigid\":{\"prompt\":\"A blanket, no-exceptions refusal is deontology at its most rigid: it forgoes a large, achievable benefit even when a consent-respecting path exists. The honest output is a named, attributable judgment, not reflexive prohibition.\",\"feedback\":\"Look for the rights-respecting route instead.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Counterarguments and Limits of Triangulation\n\nTriangulation is powerful, but it is not a verdict machine, and treating it as one introduces its own failure modes.\n\n- **It does not aggregate the lenses for you.** A three-to-one split among frameworks is not a vote. A single framework can outweigh three others if the harm it identifies is severe enough (a clear rights violation can defeat a large but diffuse aggregate benefit). The frameworks structure the argument; they do not settle it.\n- **Frameworks can be gamed.** A team that has already decided to ship can run a shallow pass of each lens and declare \"convergence.\" Honest triangulation requires adversarial effort: actively look for the strongest objection each framework can raise.\n- **The four are not exhaustive.** Justice-based frameworks (Rawls), capability approaches (Sen, Nussbaum), and many non-Western traditions (for example, *Ubuntu* relational ethics) add lenses this set omits. Four is a working minimum, not a complete toolkit.\n- **Disagreement can stall action.** Sometimes a decision must be made under genuine, unresolved framework conflict. The method's honest output in that case is \"this is a contested call by a named decision-maker,\" not silence.\n\nThe value of the method is not that it removes hard judgment. It is that it makes the judgment **visible, attributable, and reviewable** instead of hiding it inside one comfortable framework.\n\nThe single most misunderstood claim in this module is the difference between convergence and disagreement. Put it in your own words before the summary does it for you.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain the rule \\\"convergence is permission to proceed, but disagreement is the live decision,\\\" and why a three-to-one split among the frameworks is NOT a vote that the majority wins.\" answer=\"When all four lenses point the same way, no major kind of harm is being ignored, so proceeding is defensible. When they split, the disagreement marks exactly the value conflict the team cannot dodge: that is where human judgment is actually being exercised and must be documented. A three-to-one split is not a vote because the frameworks are not equal ballots; they are detectors for different severities of harm. A single severe objection, such as a clear rights violation flagged by deontology, can rightly outweigh three mild endorsements. So the method structures the argument and surfaces the conflict, but a named decision-maker, not a tally, settles it.\"></self-explain>\n\nThe five core ideas of this module form a system: four lenses feeding one triangulation method. Map how each framework's diagnostic question contributes a distinct kind of evidence to the triangulation step.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"consequentialism\",\"deontology\",\"virtue_ethics\",\"care_ethics\",\"ethical_triangulation\"]'></concept-map>\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- **One tool can earn four different verdicts.** Each framework is sensitive to a different kind of harm, so a single-lens analysis inherits that lens's blind spots.\n- **Consequentialism** asks about outcomes and misses concentrated harm; **deontology** asks about rights and can be rigid; **virtue ethics** asks about cultivated character and is hard to operationalize; **care ethics** asks about the vulnerable and risks paternalism.\n- **Convergence is permission; disagreement is the decision.** Where the four lenses split is exactly where human judgment must be applied and documented.\n- **Triangulation structures the argument but does not settle it.** Counting frameworks is not voting; a single severe objection can override several mild endorsements.\n- The point of the method is to make ethical judgment **visible and defensible**, not to manufacture a clean answer.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next section we move from these foundational frameworks to the **principles** that translate them into operational language for AI teams (fairness, accountability, transparency, and privacy), and we examine how those principles are sometimes used to launder hard tradeoffs into vague commitments.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A model helps 95% of its users a little but harms 5% catastrophically. The aggregate utility score is positive. Which framework most strongly resists the conclusion that the model should be deployed?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Consequentialism, because aggregate utility is the only criterion.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Deontology and care ethics, both of which resist sacrificing individuals or vulnerable subgroups for aggregate gains. Deontology frames it as a rights violation; care ethics frames it as a failure of attention to the vulnerable.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"None; all four frameworks agree on aggregation.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Consequentialism alone permits the trade. Deontology and care ethics each push back, for different reasons, and that pushback is precisely why triangulation is the defensible workflow.\"></interactive-quiz>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ethics_aggregation_blindspot","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"consequentialism","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["consequentialism","deontology","care_ethics"],"stem":"Which framework most strongly resists deploying a model that helps 95% of users a little while harming 5% catastrophically?","options":["Consequentialism alone, because aggregate utility is positive.","Deontology (rights violation) and care ethics (failure to attend to the vulnerable) both push back where consequentialist aggregation alone would permit.","Virtue ethics, because deployment is a habit.","None; all four agree on aggregation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Aggregation hides distribution. Deontology and care ethics each resist sacrificing individuals or vulnerable subgroups for aggregate gain.","misconception_id":"consequentialism_alone_suffices"},{"id":"ethics_virtue_question","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"virtue_ethics","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["virtue_ethics"],"stem":"What diagnostic question is most characteristic of virtue ethics applied to AI?","options":["Does it maximize aggregate utility?","Does it respect rights?","What habits and dispositions does this system cultivate in its users over months and years?","Is it legal?"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Virtue ethics evaluates technologies by their effect on user character and habits over time, not by per-action outcomes or rule compliance.","misconception_id":"virtue_ethics_is_just_kindness"},{"id":"ethics_care_focus","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"care_ethics","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["care_ethics"],"stem":"Care ethics is most distinctive in foregrounding which feature of a deployment context?","options":["Aggregate efficiency.","Power asymmetry, dependency, and the relationships in which vulnerable users are embedded.","Statutory compliance.","Brand reputation."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Care ethics centers the vulnerable and their relationships, where the other three frameworks treat power asymmetry as background.","misconception_id":"care_is_paternalism"},{"id":"ethics_triangulation_value","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ethical_triangulation","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ethical_triangulation"],"stem":"Why should AI ethics decisions triangulate across multiple frameworks rather than commit to one?","options":["Because each framework is objectively true on different days.","Because each framework has characteristic blind spots; convergence across frameworks signals a defensible decision while disagreement reveals the live ethical choice the team must make and document.","Because law requires it.","Because triangulation always produces consensus."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Triangulation exposes blind spots and locates the actual decision. Convergence is permission to proceed; disagreement is the live choice that must be argued, not hidden.","misconception_id":"one_framework_settles_everything"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"consequentialism_alone_suffices","pattern":"single_framework","description":"Believing aggregate utility is the only criterion for an AI deployment decision","trigger":"learner argues 'net benefit is positive, ship it'","feedback":"Aggregate metrics hide distribution. Deontology and care ethics resist sacrificing concentrated harm for aggregate gain. Triangulate.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"virtue_ethics_is_just_kindness","pattern":"framework_caricature","description":"Reducing virtue ethics to vague niceness rather than the systematic question of cultivated habits","trigger":"learner equates virtue ethics with 'be nice'","feedback":"Virtue ethics asks which habits and dispositions the technology cultivates in its users over time. It is the framework most equipped to name deskilling, manipulation, and erosion of judgment.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"care_is_paternalism","pattern":"framework_misframe","description":"Equating care ethics with paternalistic 'deciding for' the vulnerable","trigger":"learner dismisses care ethics as patronizing","feedback":"Care ethics attends to vulnerability and relationships without overriding agency. The risk of paternalism is real and the framework names it explicitly; it is not the framework's content.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"one_framework_settles_everything","pattern":"framework_loyalty","description":"Committing to one ethical framework and treating other frameworks' verdicts as noise","trigger":"learner cites only their preferred framework when frameworks disagree","feedback":"Frameworks have blind spots. Convergence across frameworks is what makes a decision defensible; disagreement is the live decision and should be documented, not hidden.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"29292cb01a8bb700f20d16be5d6b431f6b853d8cb3ec0c42c4110a727938a7ca","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}